## Introduction

Imagine we are managing a high-throughput payment gateway. We handle three types of transactions: `CreditCard`, `PayPal`, and `BankTransfer`. Our code uses a standard interface, `PaymentMethod`, and we have a service that processes these payments using a series of `if-else` blocks or a `switch` statement with a `default` case that throws an `IllegalStateException`. Everything works perfectly for months. Then, a junior developer on another team is tasked with adding `CryptoPayment`. They implement the `PaymentMethod` interface, and because the hierarchy is open, the compiler doesn't complain. The code deploys, a user attempts a Bitcoin payment, and your processing service hits that `default` branch. The system crashes, a partial transaction is left in limbo, and you spend your Saturday evening reconciling ledger entries.

This isn't a failure of testing; it is a failure of our domain model to communicate its boundaries to the compiler. Java 17 finalized a feature that changes this dynamic entirely, as described in JEP 409 — Sealed Classes. We are moving away from the old philosophy that everything should be extensible and toward a model where we explicitly define the limits of our system. After reading this, you will understand how to use sealed hierarchies to move business logic errors from runtime to compile-time, effectively using the compiler as a pair programmer that ensures you never miss an edge case again.

> Most engineers assume sealed classes are a tool for library authors to prevent inheritance, but the real issue is that open hierarchies make it impossible for the compiler to help us reason about business completeness.

## The Core Idea

For decades, we taught Java as a language of infinite extension. We built hierarchies where anyone could add a subclass at any time. This works for a GUI toolkit where you might want to add a `CustomButton`, but it is often disastrous for business logic. When we model a business process, we usually deal with a fixed set of states. A loan application is either `Draft`, `Submitted`, `UnderReview`, `Approved`, or `Rejected`. It is never "anything else."

Have you ever wondered why we spend so much time writing unit tests for 'impossible' code paths that only exist because the compiler doesn't know our business rules?

This is where the concept of "Totality" comes in. In functional programming, a total function is one that is defined for all possible inputs. By using sealed classes, we are creating "Sum Types" (part of Algebraic Data Types). A Sum Type says: "An `OrderState` is a `Pending` OR a `Shipped` OR a `Cancelled`." Because the compiler knows every possible implementation of `OrderState`, it can perform an exhaustiveness check. If we try to switch over an `OrderState` and we forget to handle `Cancelled`, the code simply will not compile.

### The Closed Circuit Analogy

Think of a standard interface as a power strip in a public library. Anyone can walk up and plug in any device—a lamp, a laptop, or a high-voltage industrial heater. You have no control over what is drawing power, and you have to design your fuses to handle an unknown future. 

In contrast, a sealed hierarchy is a closed electrical circuit in a specialized piece of medical equipment. Every component is known at the time the device is built. If you want to add a new sensor, you have to open the casing and intentionally wire it into the main board. The circuit won't close, and the device won't turn on, until every wire is properly connected. Sealed classes give us that same "closed-box" safety for our backend logic.

### Under the Hood: Devirtualization and the JIT

While the primary benefit of sealed classes is developer sanity, there is a performance story here as well. In a standard open hierarchy, when the JVM calls a method on an interface, it usually performs a virtual method lookup (vtable). It has to account for the possibility that a new class could be loaded at runtime that overrides that method. 

When we seal a class, we provide the JVM with a finite set of implementations. The Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler can use this information to perform more aggressive devirtualization. If the JIT sees that a sealed interface only has two implementations, it can replace a virtual call with a simple `if-else` check or even inline the code entirely. We are providing the runtime with a map of the world, allowing it to optimize for a static landscape rather than an ever-changing one.

## Code Walkthrough

### Modeling an Exhaustive State Machine

We will model a simple order processing system. Notice how we combine `sealed` with `records` to create a model that is both immutable and exhaustive. This pattern is becoming the gold standard for modern Java domain modeling.

```java
public sealed interface OrderStatus permits Pending, Shipped, Delivered, Cancelled {}

public record Pending(String orderId) implements OrderStatus {}
public record Shipped(String orderId, String trackingNumber) implements OrderStatus {}
public record Delivered(String orderId, LocalDateTime deliveredAt) implements OrderStatus {}
public record Cancelled(String orderId, String reason) implements OrderStatus {}

public class OrderService {
    public String getStatusMessage(OrderStatus status) {
        return switch (status) {
            case Pending p -> "Order " + p.orderId() + " is awaiting shipment.";
            case Shipped s -> "Order " + s.orderId() + " is in transit. Track: " + s.trackingNumber();
            case Delivered d -> "Order " + d.orderId() + " arrived at " + d.deliveredAt();
            case Cancelled c -> "Order " + c.orderId() + " was cancelled: " + c.reason();
            // No default clause needed or wanted!
        };
    }
}
```

In this code, the `permits` clause explicitly lists the allowed subtypes. If we were to add `Refunded` to the `permits` list but failed to add a `case Refunded` in the `switch` expression, the compiler would throw an error: `the switch expression does not cover all possible input values`. 

We intentionally omit the `default` case. This is a critical shift in mindset. In the past, we used `default` to catch unexpected types. Now, we want the compiler to tell us when a type is unexpected. By removing `default`, we ensure that if a new status is added to the domain in six months, every single service that processes statuses will break at compile-time, forcing us to handle the new logic correctly rather than discovering the gap in production.

## Where This Goes Wrong in Production

One common failure occurs during service-to-service communication when versions drift. If Service A (the producer) adds a new subtype to a sealed hierarchy and serializes it to JSON, and Service B (the consumer) is still running an older version of the code that doesn't know about that subtype, the deserialization layer will fail. While the compiler protects you within a single build, it cannot protect you across a distributed system. We must ensure our DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) use sealed hierarchies only when we have a coordinated deployment or a robust schema registry that can handle versioning.

Another trap is the "Deep Hierarchy" problem. We sometimes see engineers sealing a base class, then sealing its children, and then sealing the grandchildren. This creates a maintenance nightmare where every single file in the hierarchy needs to be touched to add a single leaf node. If your `permits` list starts to feel like a phone book, you are likely over-modeling. Sealed classes work best for flat, shallow hierarchies that represent clear business states, not as a replacement for every single interface in your system.

Finally, we see engineers keeping the `default` branch in their switches out of habit. This completely defeats the purpose of JEP 409. If you include a `default` case that throws an exception, you have moved the error back to runtime. You've effectively told the compiler, "I don't trust you to check my work, I'll take the risk of a crash instead." We must resist the urge to provide a safety net that actually acts as a trap.

In an interview setting, you might be asked: "Why would you use a sealed interface instead of an Enum?" The answer is data. Enums are great for simple constants, but sealed classes allow each state to hold its own unique, type-safe data (like the `trackingNumber` only existing on the `Shipped` state). This allows you to avoid null fields in a generic `Order` object.

## Key Takeaways

*   Remove `default` branches from `switch` expressions when using sealed classes to turn logic gaps into compiler errors.
*   Combine `sealed` interfaces with `record` implementations to create type-safe Algebraic Data Types that carry state-specific data.
*   Use the `permits` clause to define strict domain boundaries, preventing unauthorized extensions from breaking business invariants.
*   Leverage sealed hierarchies to replace the cumbersome Visitor Pattern, resulting in more readable and localized logic.
*   Restrict sealed hierarchies to shallow levels; if you need more than two levels of sealing, your domain model is likely too complex.
*   Coordinate deployments across microservices when changing sealed hierarchies to avoid deserialization failures on unknown types.
